{"corpus": ["# Synthetic Vehicle Service Guide \u2014 Battery and Electrical System IMPORTANT: Synthetic educational content; not an OEM manual. ## Battery-voltage guidance - 12.4\u201312.8 V: healthy resting-voltage range for this demo - 12.0\u201312.4 V: low - Below 12.0 V: abnormal A charging-system measurement while the engine is running should be interpreted separately from resting battery voltage. ## Possible causes of low voltage - Weak battery - Alternator/charging issue - Electrical load - Loose/corroded connection - Sensor/power supply issue Low battery voltage can create misleading sensor behavior, so electrical validation may be appropriate when several unrelated sensors become abnormal simultaneously.", "# Synthetic Vehicle Service Guide \u2014 Cooling System IMPORTANT: Synthetic educational content; not an OEM manual. ## Temperature guidance - 85\u2013105\u00b0C: normal operating range - 105\u2013110\u00b0C: warning zone - Above 110\u00b0C: overheating condition requiring prompt investigation ## Possible causes - Low coolant - Coolant leak - Thermostat malfunction - Cooling fan failure - Radiator restriction - Water-pump degradation - Temperature-sensor fault ## Diagnostic sequence 1. Verify coolant level when safe. 2. Check for visible leaks. 3. Check fan operation. 4. Inspect thermostat behavior. 5. Inspect radiator airflow/restriction. 6. Check water-pump performance. 7. Validate temperature-sensor readings. ## Predictive signal A gradual temperature increase over several trips is more significant than a single spike, especially when accompanied by reduced oil pressure or increased vibration.", "# Synthetic Vehicle Service Guide \u2014 Engine Oil Pressure IMPORTANT: This is synthetic project documentation created for an educational predictive-maintenance demo. It is NOT an OEM service manual and must not be used for real vehicle repair decisions. ## Normal operating guidance For this synthetic diesel vehicle family: - Normal warm-engine oil pressure: 2.0\u20134.5 bar - 1.5\u20132.0 bar: warning; inspect and monitor - Below 1.5 bar: critical; stop/restrict operation and inspect promptly ## Possible causes of low oil pressure 1. Low engine-oil level 2. Incorrect or degraded oil 3. Oil filter restriction 4. Oil pump degradation 5. Lubrication passage restriction 6. Internal engine/bearing wear 7. Faulty oil-pressure sensor or wiring ## Diagnostic sequence 1. Verify oil level. 2. Verify oil grade and oil condition. 3. Inspect for leaks. 4. Inspect/replace the oil filter if required. 5. Validate the oil-pressure sensor and wiring. 6. Measure mechanical oil pressure with an independent gauge. 7.", "sequence 1. Verify oil level. 2. Verify oil grade and oil condition. 3. Inspect for leaks. 4. Inspect/replace the oil filter if required. 5. Validate the oil-pressure sensor and wiring. 6. Measure mechanical oil pressure with an independent gauge. 7. Inspect the oil pump and lubrication system if pressure remains low. 8. Investigate internal wear only after simpler causes are excluded. ## Evidence interpretation A single low reading should be verified. A persistent downward trend combined with increased vibration or temperature is a stronger predictive-maintenance signal.", "# Synthetic Maintenance Schedule IMPORTANT: Synthetic educational content; not an OEM maintenance schedule. For this project: - Engine oil inspection: every 5,000 km - Engine oil/filter service: every 10,000 km - Cooling-system inspection: every 10,000 km - Battery/charging-system inspection: every 10,000 km - Comprehensive engine health inspection: every 20,000 km These intervals are demo values and must not be used as real-world manufacturer recommendations.", "# Synthetic Predictive Maintenance Decision Policy IMPORTANT: Synthetic policy for the capstone/demo only. ## Risk levels - 0\u201330: LOW - 31\u201360: MEDIUM - 61\u201380: HIGH - 81\u2013100: CRITICAL ## Critical indicators Any of the following can trigger critical review: - Oil pressure < 1.5 bar and persistent - Coolant temperature > 110\u00b0C and persistent - Strong worsening trend across multiple trips - Multiple correlated engine-health indicators abnormal ## Decision principle The system must not claim certainty of component failure from telemetry alone. It should state: - observed evidence - historical trend - possible causes - recommended verification - ML failure probability - confidence/limitations ## Human-in-the-loop Critical cases require qualified human review before a major maintenance action is authorized. ## Rejection/re-analysis If a technician rejects the recommendation or supplies new evidence, the workflow must re-run diagnosis using the new information and should explicitly explain", "## Human-in-the-loop Critical cases require qualified human review before a major maintenance action is authorized. ## Rejection/re-analysis If a technician rejects the recommendation or supplies new evidence, the workflow must re-run diagnosis using the new information and should explicitly explain what changed.", "# Synthetic Sensor Fault Handling Guide IMPORTANT: Synthetic educational content; not an OEM manual. ## Sensor-quality indicators Potential sensor/data-quality issue when: - A value is physically impossible - A sensor is stuck at one value for an unusually long period - The value changes abruptly without correlated vehicle behavior - Independent sensors strongly disagree ## Rule Do not interpret every abnormal sensor value as a mechanical failure. Validate sensor quality when the evidence is internally inconsistent. ## Example If oil pressure suddenly changes from 2.5 bar to 0.2 bar while engine vibration, temperature, RPM, and vehicle behavior remain stable, sensor/wiring verification should be considered before concluding catastrophic mechanical failure.", "# Synthetic Vehicle Service Guide \u2014 Engine Vibration IMPORTANT: Synthetic educational content; not an OEM manual. ## Vibration guidance - Below 5 mm/s: normal for this demo - 5\u20137 mm/s: warning - Above 7 mm/s: high ## Possible causes - Engine mount deterioration - Misfire - Imbalance - Rotating-component wear - Bearing/internal engine wear - Accessory or pulley problem ## Interpretation Increasing vibration together with falling oil pressure can indicate mechanical degradation, but it does not prove an internal engine failure. ## Recommended checks Review fault codes, engine balance/misfire data, mount condition, oil pressure, and mechanical measurements before authorizing major component replacement."], "sources": ["C:\\Users\\Administrator\\Documents\\prod-capstone\\capstone\\rag_docs\\battery_and_electrical.md", "C:\\Users\\Administrator\\Documents\\prod-capstone\\capstone\\rag_docs\\cooling_system.md", "C:\\Users\\Administrator\\Documents\\prod-capstone\\capstone\\rag_docs\\engine_oil_pressure.md", "C:\\Users\\Administrator\\Documents\\prod-capstone\\capstone\\rag_docs\\engine_oil_pressure.md", "C:\\Users\\Administrator\\Documents\\prod-capstone\\capstone\\rag_docs\\maintenance_intervals.md", "C:\\Users\\Administrator\\Documents\\prod-capstone\\capstone\\rag_docs\\predictive_maintenance_policy.md", "C:\\Users\\Administrator\\Documents\\prod-capstone\\capstone\\rag_docs\\predictive_maintenance_policy.md", "C:\\Users\\Administrator\\Documents\\prod-capstone\\capstone\\rag_docs\\sensor_fault_handling.md", "C:\\Users\\Administrator\\Documents\\prod-capstone\\capstone\\rag_docs\\vibration_and_engine_health.md"], "idf": {"is": 0.28768207245178085, "causes": 0.43078291609245417, "weak": 1.8971199848858813, "interpreted": 1.8971199848858813, "should": 0.5978370007556205, "voltage.": 1.8971199848858813, "guide": 0.5978370007556205, "important:": 0.28768207245178085, "measurement": 1.8971199848858813, "possible": 0.5978370007556205, "this": 0.7985076962177716, "load": 1.8971199848858813, "-": 0.28768207245178085, "when": 1.0498221244986776, "supply": 1.8971199848858813, "several": 1.3862943611198906, "synthetic": 0.28768207245178085, "an":